<p>US authorities have arrested Gerald Brown, 65, a former US Air Force fighter pilot. The Justice Department accuses him of training Chinese military personnel after he returned to the United States from China, according to officials. The arrest comes as the US sharpens its focus on whether Western military expertise and training methods are reaching China.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Timeline detail: The Justice Department said Brown had been in China since December 2023. Authorities arrested him in Indiana after he returned to the US.</li><li>Labeling in coverage: Some reports describe Brown broadly as a former US Air Force fighter pilot. Others link him more specifically to the F-35 programme.</li><li>Recruitment concern: Coverage notes the case fits a wider US concern that China has sought to recruit Western military expertise and training experience.</li><li>Case framing: One report used strong moral language such as “betraying” while attributing those claims to US accusations.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>US Justice Department: The department said Brown had been in China since December 2023 and that authorities arrested him in Indiana after he returned to the US. Prosecutors accuse him of training Chinese military personnel. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/gerald-brown-ex-us-air-force-fighter-pilot-arrested-for-training-chinese-military-personnel-11137627#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>, <a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/26/former-us-f-35-fighter-pilot-arrested-for-training-chinese-air-force'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera: The report highlighted the US allegation that Brown trained Chinese air force pilots and placed the arrest in the context of broader US-China strategic competition. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/26/former-us-f-35-fighter-pilot-arrested-for-training-chinese-air-force'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Firstpost: The outlet used blunt moral language, describing the alleged conduct as “betraying” 